<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="linear-transformation-complete">
    <title>Linear Transformations (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Another important perspective of matrices is that they represent functions between 
                        vector spaces. We'll look at the important properties of such functions, and visualize
                        specific cases.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

           <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Linear Transformations</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
              A <term>linear transformation</term> is a function <m>T: V \to W</m> such that 
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>T(c_1\vvec_1 + c_2 \vvec_2) = c_1T(\vvec_1) + c_2T(\vvec_2)</m>. Intuitively, linear transformations
                        behave nicely with linear combinations of vectors.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Examples: Linear Transformations</title>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Differentiation and Integration on a Function Space.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Properties you learned regarding derivatives and integrals from Calc 1 show that the derivative and integral
                    can be considered linear transformations on function spaces:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\left(c_1f(x) + c_2 g(x)\right) \amp= c_1\frac{d}{dx}\left(f(x)\right) + c_2\frac{d}{dx}\left(g(x)\right) </mrow>
                        <mrow>\int (c_1 f(x) + c_2 g(x)) \quad dx \amp= c_1 \int f(x) \quad dx + c_2 \int g(x) \quad dx </mrow>
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Matrix Multiplication.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix, then one can define a linear transformation <m>T: \R^n \to \R^m</m>
                    by matrix multiplication with <m>A</m>:
                    <md>
                        T(\vvec) = A \cdot \vvec
                    </md>
                    The following properties of matrices confirm this is a linear transformation:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>T(c_1 \vvec_1 + c_2 \vvec_2) \amp= A(c_1\vvec_1 + c_2\vvec_2) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp= c_1(A\vvec_1)+ c_2(A\vvec_2) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp=c_1T(\vvec_1)+ c_2T(\vvec_2) </mrow>
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
                <p>
                    Conversely, every linear transformation between finite-dimensional vector spaces can be 
                    represented by a matrix! In particular, if <m>T: V \to W</m> and <m>V</m> has basis 
                    <m>\{\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \dots, \vvec_n\}</m>, then
                    <md>
                        T(\vvec) = \left[T(\vvec_1) \,\, T(\vvec_2) \,\, \dots \,\, T(\vvec_n) \right] \cdot \vvec
                    </md>.
                    That is, <m>T</m> is the transformation defined by multiplication with the matrix whose columns are obtained 
                    by <m>T</m> acting on each basis vector of <m>V</m>.
                    
                </p>
                <p>
                    You can use the <url href="https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/jsactivities/matrixmult.html">matrix multiplication interactive</url>
                    to visualize some transformations from <m>\R^2 \to \R^2</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
    <title>Remarks: Linear Transformations</title>
    <task workspace="6in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Linear transformations and Matrix Properties.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Viewing matrices as linear transformations can help us with otherwise abstract properties of matrices.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Inverses. We can recognize that the matrix
                            <md> A = 
                                \begin{bmatrix}
                                0 \amp 1 \\
                                -1 \amp 0
                                \end{bmatrix}
                            </md>
                            is invertible with inverse matrix 
                            <md> A^{-1} = 
                                \begin{bmatrix}
                                0 \amp -1 \\
                                1 \amp 0
                                \end{bmatrix}
                            </md>
                            by recognizing that <m>A</m> is the transformation that rotates all vectors <m>90</m> degrees in the 
                            clockwise direction. This is an invertible transformation, which is rotation <m>90</m> degrees in the
                            counter-clockwise direction.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Determinants. <m>\text{det}(A)</m> is the scaling factor of areas under the transformation <m>A</m>. 
                            In particular, transformations that collapse vectors to one dimension have determinant zero, and 
                            transformations that reverses the orientation of space have negative determinant. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="6in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Linear transformations and Changing Bases.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Doing a basis change for a vector space is a linear transformation. For example, if we'd like to do a basis 
                change in <m>\R^2</m> from the basis <m>\mathcal{B} = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>
                to the standard basis, we'd form the matrix (linear transformation)
                <md>
                    A = [T(\evec_1) \,\, T(\evec_2)] = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>,
                which is restating that <m>\evec_1</m> in <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates means <m>1 \cdot \begin{bmatrix}1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} + 0 \cdot \begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}  </m>, and 
                similar for <m>\evec_2</m>.
                
            </p>
            <p>
                If <m>A</m> is the change of basis matrix from <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates to standard coordinates, then <m>A^{-1}</m> 
                is the change of basis matrix from standard coordinates to <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    
        
    </exercise>
</page>




       
    </worksheet>
